  "start": 1200.08,
  "end": 1373.45,
  "text": "Like twelve years, actually fourteen years. There were two years we rented a house on Country Hill, and now we have a house on Darby Street. And I've really enjoyed living here. It's a great place. It satisfies all my needs. It's quiet. It's safe. It's clean. I enjoy the parks. I go hiking all the time. I like the university. And, you know, it's been a great deal. So I have really no complaints. It's been very well-run, in my impression, even though I haven't really kept up the details. But I feel like the place has been well-run, and I really appreciate of that. You know, it's been a fairly conservatively, you know, as far as, you know, spending money, it's been very, very careful of how you spend money and everything like that. So that's my original proclamation. However, I mean, I've known that there's this huge school bond, which I voted against, you know, hundreds of millions of dollars to borrow. And then, you know, I get this letter about our assessments. And, I mean, frankly, you know, while we lived here, our taxes maybe would go up, I don't know, a few percent a year, okay? Mostly due to the rising assessments, not the rate. The rate has changed a little bit, but very small. But then I get this little, you know, yellow bill, which I guess we all got, not a bill, but... And my taxes, the total amount I'm going to end up paying in 2025, if this is all approved, has gone up 20% in one single year. I mean, I hope everybody... I mean, and I'm just flabbergasted, because until now, nothing like this has ever happened. Everything has been very regular, very small. Obviously, I think, you know, you have to live within your means. Now, our assessments rose. That's always happened so far. I mean, it doesn't mean it always will, but it has. And that's really helped keep the budget, you know, the amount of money that you get with inflation. But to actually increase the rate from $1.03 to $1.1250, and then, I understand, it's really not $1.1250. They, actually, there's talk about raising it to $1.15. I mean, I just don't understand this at all. I think it's way, you know, like somebody said, you know, we're living in a very tough time. We need to live within our means. I'm against this completely, against raising the rate, totally. And my proposal would be, why not just live on the increased assessments? So I guess my little red light is on?"
